{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And he said: Your name shall no longer be called Jacob, but Israel.</b> This is the language of יָשָׁר אֵל, 'straight toward El,' for יָשָׁר is a term of seeing, from the language of ״אֲשׁוּרֶנּוּ וְלֹא קָרוֹב״ (Numbers 24:17), and thereby he acknowledged him — that Jacob sees the face of El and was unable to blind him through the perception of El, blessed be He. And in his saying ״כִּי שָׂרִיתָ עִם אֱלֹהִים״ ('for you have striven with Elohim'), he uprooted the name Jacob from him, for Jacob alludes to ״עָקֹב הַלֵּב מִכֹּל וְאָנֻשׁ הוּא״ (Jeremiah 17:9), whereas Israel is a term of מִישׁוֹר, straightness, as it is written concerning the future (Isaiah 40:4): ״וְהָיָה הֶעָקֹב לְמִישׁוֹר״ — 'and the crooked shall become straight.' And not the straightness that appears upright in the eyes of creatures, but rather that which appears upright in the eyes of Elohim and man; therefore he said ״כִּי שָׂרִיתָ עִם אֱלֹהִים וְעִם אֲנָשִׁים״ ('for you have striven with Elohim and with men') — for through the uprightness of your deeds you shall be a prince and ruler with Elohim and with men, and you shall prevail. And this is Israel — יָשָׁר אֵל, the straightness that appears upright also in the eyes of El, blessed be He."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:45.732275645Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}